TITLE: How playing-card localization works
DATE: 6 May 2026
ISO_DATE: 2026-05-06

<article>
  <p class="meta">
    <span title="Published">6 May 2026</span>
    <span class="divider"></span>
    <span title="Time to read">~8 mins</span>
    <span class="divider"></span>
    <span title="Tags">Computer vision, ML, Localization</span>
  </p>

  <p>
    For today, here is more detail on how the card localization pipeline works: two model outputs drive corner
    detection and grouping, then homographies rectify each card for downstream recognition.
  </p>

  <h4>Model outputs</h4>
  <p>
    The image is first processed by a model with two heads: a <strong>corner heatmap</strong> and a
    <strong>segmentation embedding</strong>. Below are placeholders for sample visualizations of each.
  </p>
  <p><em>Image placeholder: corner heatmap (grayscale; white blob per corner).</em></p>
  <p><em>Image placeholder: segmentation embedding (colorful; each card gets a consistent color in embedding space).</em></p>
  <p>
    The corner heatmap is black and white: a bright blob marks each corner. The segmentation head is colorful in
    visualization—it effectively assigns a color (embedding vector) to each card region so corners on the same card
    should land in similar color space.
  </p>

  <h4>1. Extract corners from the heatmap</h4>
  <p>
    The heatmap is thresholded; the center of each white blob becomes one entry in an unordered list of corner
    locations. For each corner, the pipeline samples values from the other outputs (segmentation embedding and
    orientation heatmap) at that pixel location.
  </p>

  <h4>2. Sample segmentation and orientation at each corner</h4>
  <p>
    At every corner, the segmentation embedding is read with bilinear interpolation. That assigns an embedding
    “color” to the corner. When segmentation is healthy, all four corners of the same card get similar vectors.
  </p>
  <p><em>Image placeholder: heatmap with corner overlays.</em></p>
  <p><em>Image placeholder: embedding visualization at corner samples.</em></p>
  <p><em>Image placeholder: orientation heatmap sample.</em></p>

  <h4>3. Group corners into quads</h4>
  <p>
    <strong>Version one</strong> was a greedy pairwise merge: if the color distance between two corners fell below a
    threshold, merge them; when a group reached four corners, close it. It was fast, but a single threshold could not
    cover all scenes—either true corners stayed split (threshold too tight) or unrelated corners merged (too loose).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Version two</strong> casts a wider net. All corner pairs closer than a cutoff become candidate
    <em>edges</em>. Every pair of edges yields a candidate quadrilateral of four corners; the sum of edge color
    distances is the candidate score. Candidates are sorted by that score (lower is more self-consistent). A greedy
    pass walks the sorted list and accepts a candidate only if none of its four corners were already used—so later
    high-score quads that reuse corners are skipped.
  </p>
  <p>The debug log looks roughly like this:</p>
  <pre><code>num candidates 22
score 0.122459, 0, 1, 8, 9
group
score 0.225621, 3, 7, 10, 15
group
score 0.683872, 3, 6, 10, 15 &lt;&lt; Skipped because corner 3, 10, 15 already used
score 0.684685, 0, 8, 9, 10 &lt;&lt; Skipped because corner 0, 8, 9 already used
score 0.691545, 5, 6, 4, 2
group
...
Found 3 Cards</code></pre>

  <h4>4. Homography and per-card inference</h4>
  <p>
    Once corners are grouped into fours, a homography maps each quadrilateral to a canonical rectangle, removing
    perspective and cropping tightly. That warp is run for every accepted group, then recognition runs on each
    rectified card crop.
  </p>

  <h4>Performance and training data</h4>
  <p>
    In practice it is fairly reliable on the order of 12–16 cards when the camera angle is not extreme. The main
    bottleneck so far is training realism: the synthetic generator emits three families of layouts—fully random
    position and rotation, random position with similar orientation, and a binder-style grid. Lighting is the weakest
    link; cards tend to float in space, and stronger shadows and ambient occlusion would likely help generalization.
  </p>

